<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maluku Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Moluccas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maluku Islands, or Moluccas, are an archipelago in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, historically known as the Spice Islands. They were the original source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> and mace, which were highly prized in global trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> seized control of the spice trade there in the seventeenth century, making it a cornerstone of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>'s economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maluku-islands.jpg" alt="Maluku Islands" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lencer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maluku_Islands_en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 1 January 1800 its territories, centred on Batavia and parts of Java, the <b>Moluccas</b> and scattered trading posts, passed to the Batavian Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The spice monopolies enforced across the <b>Maluku Islands</b>, covering nutmeg, cloves and mace, were maintained through deliberate violence, including the Dutch conquest of the Banda Islands under Jan Pieterszoon Coen in 1621, with the near-extermination and enslavement of their population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Communal conflicts in <b>Maluku</b> and Kalimantan and the Bali bombings of 2002 tested the new democracy, and the Indian Ocean tsunami of December 2004 killed well over 100,000 people in Aceh, a disaster that helped bring the Helsinki peace agreement with the Free Aceh Movement in August 2005.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Maritime trade was central to the empire's wealth, linking ports from Sumatra and Borneo to Sulawesi and <b>Maluku</b> with international networks reaching China, India and the Malay Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ships from China, the Ryukyu Islands, Java and the <b>Maluku Islands</b> came in on the northeast monsoon from December to January, and ships for the Indian coasts, the Red Sea and East Africa left on the southwest monsoon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…met by slave raiding: Iranun and Balangingi Sama fleets based in the Sulu zone seized, by his estimates, thousands of captives a year from the Visayas, Luzon, the <b>Moluccas</b>, Sulawesi and the Malay world, many of whom were set to fishing and collecting marine produce or sold onward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
